"""Narrative retellings shown under each dialogue in the library.

The date line stays separate. These are what happens in the work.
Word counts are checked when the catalogue pages are written.
"""

STORY = {
    "parmenides": (
        "Cephalus of Clazomenae meets Adeimantus and Glaucon in the Athenian market and asks after their brother Antiphon, who once learned a conversation by heart. At Antiphon's house the old story is told. During the Great Panathenaea, Parmenides and Zeno were staying with Pythodorus. Zeno read aloud his book of arguments against the claim that things are many. Socrates, still very young, answered that things are many because they share in forms, while each form is one. Parmenides then took that answer apart. If a form is separate from the things that share in it, how do they share, and how could anyone know the form? He said Socrates still needed training, and he set him an exercise. Aristotle, the young man who would later be one of the Thirty, answered while Parmenides worked through the consequences of saying that the one is, and of saying that the one is not, both for the one and for the others. Hypothesis after hypothesis ended in opposite conclusions. Aristotle agreed at last that, take it as you will, the one and the others both are and are not, and both seem and do not seem, in every way. The dialogue stops on that result."
    ),
    "protagoras": (
        "Before dawn Hippocrates bangs on Socrates' door, thrilled that the famous Protagoras is in Athens, and begs to be taken to him. They talk in the courtyard until it is light, then go to Callias' house. The doorkeeper nearly shuts them out, mistaking them for more sophists. Inside, Protagoras walks up and down with a crowd of followers. Hippias is teaching in one room, and Prodicus is still in bed in another, his deep voice booming. Socrates asks Protagoras, in front of everyone, whether virtue can be taught. Protagoras answers with the myth of Prometheus and Epimetheus and a long speech: the gods gave all citizens a share of justice, so the Athenians are right to let any man advise the city. Socrates then presses him on whether justice, piety, and courage are one thing or many. They turn to a poem of Simonides and argue over what it means to become good. Socrates next argues that courage is a kind of knowledge, because no one chooses what he believes will harm him. By the end each man has landed near the position the other began with. Socrates says they ought to go on, and they part."
    ),
    "alcibiades-1": (
        "Socrates meets Alcibiades and says he has not spoken to him all these years because a divine sign held him back, and the sign has only now let him approach. Alcibiades is about to address the Assembly and is sure he can advise Athens on war and peace. Socrates asks what he would be advising about, and what he knows that the Assembly does not. Alcibiades says he knows what is just, then admits he never learned it and never found he lacked it. Socrates leads him to say that the just and the advantageous are the same, and that he has been answering from his own confusion. The talk turns to what it is to care for oneself rather than for one's belongings. The user of a thing is different from the thing he uses, so the man is the soul that uses the body. To know oneself is to look into another soul, as an eye sees itself in the pupil of an eye. Alcibiades agrees that he must be educated before he rules, and that if the city corrupts him it will be the stronger of the two."
    ),
    "charmides": (
        "Socrates comes back from the camp at Potidaea and goes straight to Taureas' palaestra. Chaerephon greets him, and Critias tells him about the beautiful Charmides, who is said to be temperate as well. Charmides enters and the room turns to look. Socrates is asked to pretend he has a cure for the boy's headache. Looking inside Charmides' cloak, he is thrown off balance, then recovers and says the charm only works if the soul is treated first. He questions Charmides on what temperance is. The boy tries quietness, then modesty, then doing one's own work, and Critias takes over the last of these. Critias offers self-knowledge, and then a knowledge that knows itself and every other knowledge. Socrates shows that such a knowledge would not, by itself, give us the knowledge of good and bad that actually benefits us. They reach no definition. Critias tells Charmides to attach himself to Socrates, and Charmides says he will use force if he has to. Socrates says there is no resisting the two of them."
    ),
    "gorgias": (
        "Socrates and Chaerephon arrive late at Callicles' house, just after Gorgias has given a rhetorical display, and they persuade him to answer questions instead. Gorgias says rhetoric produces persuasion in law courts and assemblies about the just and the unjust, not knowledge of those things. Pressed on whether the orator must know justice, he is tangled, and Polus bursts in to defend the art as a fine thing that gives a man the power of a tyrant. Socrates answers that rhetoric is a knack of flattery, like cookery, not a real art of the soul, and that doing wrong is worse than suffering it. Callicles then takes over and mocks philosophy as a pastime for boys. He says nature lets the stronger take more, and that pleasure is the good. Socrates drives him through the consequences, including a life spent scratching itches, and distinguishes pleasures that are good from those that are not. The talk ends with a myth: naked souls are judged after death, and the only safety is to have practised justice. Socrates tells Callicles to follow that course with him."
    ),
    "laches": (
        "Lysimachus, son of Aristides, and Melesias, son of Thucydides, have watched a man display fighting in armour and ask Nicias and Laches whether their own sons should learn it. They are ashamed that their famous fathers left them no distinction. The two generals disagree, and Lysimachus turns to Socrates, who was with them at the retreat from Delium. Socrates says the question is not the instructor but the thing the boys are meant to acquire, and that thing is the care of their souls. They must therefore say what courage is. Laches tries standing one's ground in battle, then a kind of endurance. Socrates shows that some courageous acts are retreats, and that endurance without wisdom is not courage. Nicias offers knowledge of what is and is not to be feared, for a person and for a city. That account spreads until it seems to cover all of virtue, and Nicias cannot keep it to courage alone. They are all at a loss. Socrates says they should look for a teacher, and the others agree to meet him again."
    ),
    "lysis": (
        "Socrates is walking from the Academy to the Lyceum when Hippothales calls him over to a newly built palaestra. Hippothales is in love with the boy Lysis, writes poems about him and his ancestors, and blushes when Ctesippus tells on him. Socrates says that kind of praise only makes the beloved harder to catch. They go inside. Socrates talks with Lysis about why his parents, who love him, still will not let him do as he likes: they trust him where he has knowledge and restrain him where he does not. He then calls over Menexenus and asks what a friend is. They try out likeness, unlikeness, the presence of the good, and desire for what one lacks. Each account breaks. The servants come to take the boys home. Socrates gets in a last word: he and the two boys seem to be friends, and the others think they are, and yet they have not been able to discover what a friend is."
    ),
    "hippias-major": (
        "Hippias of Elis arrives boastful. He has been serving his city as an envoy, making money by his speeches, and he looks down on the old wise men who never earned a fee. Socrates says he envies that confidence, because a man at home keeps asking him a blunt question and laughing at his answers. The question is what the beautiful is. Hippias first offers a beautiful girl, then gold, then to be rich, healthy, honoured, to bury one's parents, and to be buried by one's children. Socrates, speaking for the man at home, shows that each of these is an example or a case, not what beauty is in itself. They try the fitting, the useful, the favourable, and the pleasant that comes through eye and ear. Each proposal fails, sometimes because it leaves out beautiful things and sometimes because it cannot say what the pleasures have in common. Hippias grows angry at the quibbling. Socrates ends with the proverb that beautiful things are difficult."
    ),
    "hippias-minor": (
        "Hippias has just spoken on Homer, and Socrates keeps him to ask about one claim: that Achilles is better than Odysseus because Achilles is straight and Odysseus is wily. Hippias agrees that the truthful man and the false man are different people. Socrates argues from calculation, geometry, and astronomy that the same person is best able both to tell the truth and to lie about a subject, namely the one who knows it. He then asks whether a runner who runs slowly on purpose is a better runner than one who cannot help it, and he pushes the same pattern onto doing wrong. Hippias is outraged at the idea that a person who does wrong willingly could be better than one who does it against his will. Socrates says the argument keeps dragging him there, and that he vacillates, sometimes agreeing and sometimes not. They part without a settled answer."
    ),
    "phaedrus": (
        "Phaedrus has spent the morning with Lysias and is walking outside the walls to learn by heart a speech Lysias wrote. Socrates meets him, sees he is hiding the scroll, and makes him read it under a plane tree by the Ilissus. The speech argues that a boy should grant his favours to a man who is not in love with him, because a lover is jealous, unsteady, and damaging. Socrates, claiming he can do better, covers his head and gives a speech of his own to the same effect. As he is about to cross the stream, his divine sign stops him. He recants. Love, he now says, is a madness sent by the gods. The soul is like a charioteer with two horses, one noble and one unruly. Souls that follow a god around the rim of heaven glimpse the forms, and when they fall and are reborn the sight of a beautiful person makes their wings begin to grow again. Cicadas sing over the two men, and Socrates says the insects are watching to see whether they talk through the noon or fall asleep. They then judge Lysias' speech as rhetoric. A good speech needs an order, like a living body, and the speaker needs to know souls the way a doctor knows bodies. Writing, they agree, is only a reminder, weaker than talk that can answer. Socrates prays to Pan as they leave."
    ),
    "symposium": (
        "Apollodorus is stopped on the road by friends who want the story of Agathon's party. He had told it recently to Glaucon, and he had it from Aristodemus, who was there. On the night after Agathon won his first prize, Aristodemus met Socrates washed and in sandals, a rare sight, and was invited along. Socrates fell behind, standing on a neighbour's porch, and Aristodemus arrived alone. When Socrates came in they dined. Pausanias proposed that they drink lightly, having been drunk the night before, and send the flute girl away, each man giving a speech in praise of love. Phaedrus speaks of love as the oldest god and the source of honour. Pausanias divides a vulgar love from a heavenly one. Eryximachus, after curing Aristophanes' hiccups, makes love a principle of harmony in bodies and in the cosmos. Aristophanes tells of round people split in half, each half searching for its other. Agathon gives a polished praise of the god. Socrates says he cannot praise in that style. He reports the priestess Diotima: love is not beautiful or wise but a daimon between gods and mortals, child of Poverty and Resource, and it is the desire to possess the good forever. A lover can climb from one beautiful body to all beautiful bodies, then to souls, laws, and knowledge, and at last to beauty itself. Alcibiades then crashes in, drunk and crowned, and instead of praising love he praises Socrates, telling of the night Socrates refused his seduction, and of Potidaea and the retreat from Delium. A crowd of revellers bursts in. Near dawn Socrates is still arguing that the same man should be able to write tragedy and comedy. The others fall asleep. He gets up, spends the day as usual, and goes home in the evening."
    ),
    "ion": (
        "Socrates meets the rhapsode Ion of Ephesus, who has just won the prize at the festival of Asclepius in Epidaurus and is confident about Homer. Socrates asks whether Ion is equally clever about Hesiod and the other poets. Ion says he nods off when anyone speaks of them, and comes awake only for Homer. Socrates argues that if speaking well about poetry were a craft, the man who had it could handle every poet, since the craft is one. He then takes the passages where Homer talks of chariot racing, medicine, fishing, and generalship, and asks who can judge those lines, the rhapsode or the specialist. Ion claims that a good rhapsode is also a good general, and that he could command. Socrates says a man who can really do that would not be content to recite. He offers another account: a poet is held by a divine power, like an iron ring on a magnet, and the rhapsode and the audience are further rings in the same chain. Ion prefers to be called divinely possessed rather than dishonest. Socrates grants him the finer title."
    ),
    "euthydemus": (
        "Crito asks Socrates who the two men were that he saw him talking with the day before in the Lyceum, inside a crowd. Socrates says they were Euthydemus and Dionysodorus, brothers who used to teach fighting in armour and now claim to teach virtue, and to refute whatever anyone says. He narrates the scene. Young Clinias was there, and Ctesippus, who is fond of him. The brothers put Clinias through traps: if he knows one thing he knows everything, the one who learns is the one who does not know, and so on, until Ctesippus grows angry and starts answering in the same style. Between their displays Socrates questions Clinias himself, urging him toward wisdom as the only good thing that cannot be misused, and asking what art would both produce good things and know how to use them. They never quite find that art. Afterwards a man who heard the display, a writer of courtroom speeches, sneers at philosophy to Crito. Socrates tells Crito not to judge the thing by its bad practitioners, and Crito goes away still worried about how to educate his sons."
    ),
    "republic": (
        "Socrates goes down to the Piraeus for the first festival of the Thracian goddess Bendis. On the way back, Polemarchus sends a slave to stop him, and Socrates spends the evening at the house of Cephalus, Polemarchus' father. The old man talks about wealth and old age and leaves to sacrifice. The conversation becomes an argument about justice. Polemarchus says it is helping friends and harming enemies. Socrates shows that a just man does not harm anyone. Thrasymachus breaks in, angry, and defines justice as the advantage of the stronger, the interest of whatever party rules. Socrates answers that rulers can mistake their interest, and that every craft aims at the good of its subject, not of the craftsman. Glaucon and Adeimantus are not satisfied. Glaucon tells of Gyges' ring, which makes a man invisible, and demands a proof that justice is worth having for itself, even if the just man is thought wicked and the unjust man is thought good. To see justice written in larger letters, Socrates builds a city in speech. He describes its guardians, their education in music and gymnastics, the noble lie about metals in the soul, and the life of the rulers, who hold property and families in common. The city will be saved only if philosophers rule or rulers become philosophers. He explains the philosopher by the images of the sun, the divided line, and the cave, then traces how cities and souls decline through oligarchy and democracy into tyranny. The tyrant, enslaved to appetite, is the most miserable of men. The last book argues that the soul is immortal and closes with the myth of Er: souls choose their next lives, and the choice is everything."
    ),
    "timaeus": (
        "Socrates meets Timaeus of Locri, Critias, and Hermocrates the day after he has described to them a city like the one in the Republic, and he asks them to repay him by showing that city at war. Critias says he can do it. He heard from his grandfather a story that Solon brought from Egypt: the priests at Sais told Solon of an Athens that existed nine thousand years before, and of Atlantis, an island power that attacked it and was afterwards swallowed by the sea. Before that history, Timaeus, who knows astronomy, agrees to speak of the making of the world. He distinguishes what always is from what comes to be, and says the cosmos was made by a good craftsman who wanted everything to be as like himself as possible. The world is a single living creature, with a soul stretched through it, and time is the wandering of the heavenly bodies. Timaeus then describes necessity as well as reason: the receptacle in which things appear, the elements, the construction of the human body, the placement of the soul's parts, and the diseases of body and soul. He ends by saying how the souls of cowardly or light-minded men are reborn as women and then as animals, until the account of the universe is complete down to mortal creatures."
    ),
    "critias": (
        "After Timaeus finishes, Critias asks indulgence for the difficulty of speaking about human beings, which we judge more strictly than we judge an account of the gods, and he prays to Memory. He then tells the story he had promised. The gods once divided the earth by lot. Athena and Hephaestus received Athens, and Poseidon received Atlantis. Ancient Athens, on a larger acropolis than the present one, kept a guardian class apart from the craftsmen and farmers, and lived simply. Atlantis was a vast island. Poseidon's descendants cut concentric rings of sea and land around the hill where he had settled, bridged them, and filled the plain with canals, temples, and wealth. For a long time they obeyed the laws and prized virtue above gold. Then the divine portion in them faded, they became greedy, and Zeus decided to punish them. He called the gods together in his palace. The text breaks off as Zeus is about to speak."
    ),
    "theages": (
        "Demodocus of the deme Anagyrus comes into the city with his son Theages and finds Socrates. The boy wants to become wise, by which he means great among the Athenians, and he has been nagging his father to place him with a sophist. Socrates asks what he wants to learn, and shows him that the companions of Pericles and the other politicians have not picked up their skill by sitting beside them. Theages still wants Socrates himself. Socrates says he has no craft to teach. What he has is a divine sign, a voice that from childhood has turned him away from things he is about to do, and that also decides which of his companions are helped by being with him. Some improve the whole time they stay, and lose the benefit when they leave. Theages proposes that they try the association and see what the sign does. The dialogue ends on that proposal."
    ),
    "alcibiades-2": (
        "Socrates meets Alcibiades as he is walking to pray, and asks whether he has considered the danger of the prayer he is about to make. People often beg the gods for marriage, office, or the death of an enemy, and then curse the day they received it. He reminds him of Oedipus, whose prayer that his sons divide the inheritance by the sword was fulfilled in the worst way. They talk about a kind of ignorance that is worse than ordinary ignorance: not knowing that one does not know what is good. Socrates praises the Spartans, who ask the gods only for what is noble and good, and leave the choice of the gift to them. Alcibiades is persuaded to put the sacrifice off. He takes the garland he had prepared and places it on Socrates instead."
    ),
    "meno": (
        "Meno, a young Thessalian staying with Anytus, asks Socrates straight off whether virtue is taught, or acquired by practice, or present in people by nature. Socrates says he cannot answer, because he does not know what virtue is, and he has never met anyone who does. Meno offers definitions: a man's virtue is ruling, a woman's is running the house, and so on for child, slave, and old man. Socrates asks for the one thing they all share. Meno tries colour-like accounts and then complains that Socrates has numbed him, as a stingray numbs its prey. He asks how anyone can search for what he does not know at all. Socrates answers with recollection: the soul has learned before this life, and questioning draws the knowledge out. He calls over one of Meno's slaves and, by questions alone, brings the boy to see how to double a square, using the diagonal. Anytus joins them. He despises the sophists and says any decent Athenian can teach virtue. Socrates points out that the great men, Themistocles, Aristides, Pericles, and Thucydides, did not manage to teach it to their own sons. If there are no teachers, virtue is not knowledge. It may be true opinion, given by a divine allotment, like the insight of prophets. Anytus is offended. Socrates tells Meno to stay and calm him down."
    ),
    "menexenus": (
        "Menexenus comes from the Council chamber, where the Athenians are about to choose the speaker for the public funeral of the war dead. He thinks he is now old enough to hold office. Socrates says there is no great trick to funeral oratory: the speaker praises the city whether or not the dead deserved it, and Athens supplies ready-made praises. He claims to have learned a specimen from Aspasia, who he says also composed the famous speech of Pericles, and who has just gone over this one with him. Menexenus dares him to recite it, and he does, after warning that he may be laughed at. The speech traces the Athenians from their birth out of the Attic soil, through the Persian wars, the conflicts among the Greeks, and on to wars later than Socrates' own death. It consoles the parents and sons of the dead and sends them home. Menexenus is impressed and asks to hear more of Aspasia's politics. Socrates agrees, provided Menexenus tells nobody."
    ),
    "cratylus": (
        "Hermogenes and Cratylus have been arguing, and Hermogenes asks Socrates to join them. Cratylus has been saying that Hermogenes is not really his name. Hermogenes holds that names are conventional: whatever a person or a city agrees to call a thing is its name. Cratylus holds that names belong to things by nature, and that a badly made name is not a name at all. Socrates tries the question as a toolmaker would. A name is an instrument for teaching and for separating being, and the man who makes names is a legislator who looks to the form of the name. He then offers a long run of etymologies, of gods, of elements, of virtues, and of love, many of them suggesting that the first name-givers believed everything is in flux. Hermogenes is delighted. Socrates then turns on that picture. If everything constantly flows, there is nothing stable to name and nothing stable to know. He also gets Cratylus to admit that some names are poorly made, and that if names can be wrong we cannot learn the truth about things simply by studying their names. Better to look at the things themselves. Cratylus still leans toward Heraclitus. They agree to think more and talk again."
    ),
    "theaetetus": (
        "Euclid of Megara meets Terpsion and tells him that Theaetetus has been carried up from the camp at Corinth, badly wounded, and is dying. Euclid remembers a conversation Socrates had with Theaetetus when Theaetetus was a boy, which Socrates later recounted and Euclid wrote down. A slave reads the book. In it, Theodorus of Cyrene introduces Theaetetus, who looks like Socrates and is remarkable at mathematics. Socrates says he practises the art of his mother, Phaenarete the midwife, except that he delivers souls. He asks what knowledge is. Theaetetus first says it is geometry, cobbling, and the other crafts. Socrates wants the one thing they share. Theaetetus then tries perception. Socrates develops that answer through Protagoras, for whom each man is the measure of what is, and through Heraclitus, for whom everything flows, and then shows that if perception is knowledge we cannot even say what a person will experience next, or distinguish dreaming from waking in the way the theory needs. Theaetetus next tries true judgment, and then true judgment together with an account. Socrates compares the mind to a wax block and to an aviary, and he takes apart three meanings of giving an account. None of the definitions survives. Socrates says he must go to the King's Porch to meet the indictment Meletus has drawn up, and he asks Theodorus to meet him again in the morning."
    ),
    "euthyphro": (
        "Socrates is at the porch of the King Archon, about to answer the charge Meletus has brought, that he corrupts the young and invents new gods. He meets Euthyphro, who is there to prosecute his own father. A dependent of the family had killed a household slave in a drunken fight on Naxos. Euthyphro's father bound the man and threw him in a ditch while he sent to Athens to ask the religious authority what to do, and the prisoner died of neglect. The relatives say a son should not prosecute a father. Euthyphro is sure that he knows piety better than they do. Socrates asks to become his pupil, so that he can use the knowledge at his own trial. Euthyphro says piety is doing what he is doing, prosecuting a wrongdoer whoever it is. Socrates wants the form, the one thing that makes every pious act pious. Euthyphro says it is what the gods love. Socrates asks what happens when the gods quarrel. Euthyphro narrows it to what all the gods love. Socrates asks whether the gods love the pious because it is pious, or whether it is pious because they love it, and Euthyphro cannot keep the two apart. They try piety as a care of the gods, and as a part of justice, the part concerned with service to the gods, and they cannot say what that service produces. Euthyphro remembers he is in a hurry and leaves. Socrates protests that he has not learned what he hoped."
    ),
    "sophist": (
        "Theodorus keeps the appointment made the day before and brings with him a stranger from Elea, a companion of the circle of Parmenides and Zeno. Socrates asks whether the visitor is a god in disguise, come to examine them, and then requests that he tell them what the sophist, the statesman, and the philosopher are, and whether the three names belong to one kind or to three. The stranger chooses Theaetetus as his partner and begins with the sophist. They divide arts, productive and acquisitive, hunting and exchange, and they corner a figure who deals in speeches, claims to dispute about everything, and produces appearances. The definition sticks on a problem Parmenides had forbidden them to face: a copy and a false statement seem to say what is not. The stranger argues that this prohibition cannot stand. He sets out the greatest kinds, being, rest, motion, sameness, and difference, and shows that difference lets us say that a thing is not, meaning that it is other than something else. A statement weaves a verb together with a name, and it is false when it says of what is something other than what is about it. With false speech possible, they can finish the hunt. The sophist is an imitator who works in private, by short question and answer, knowing that he does not know. The stranger says they must not relax until the statesman has been caught as well."
    ),
    "statesman": (
        "The Eleatic stranger continues at once, and young Socrates takes Theaetetus' place as the one who answers. They look for the statesman by dividing the art of herding living things, cutting down through land animals and two-footed animals until the human herd is left. The division has put the statesman in with merchants, farmers, doctors, and every other claimant who helps feed the herd. To correct the picture the stranger tells a myth. The cosmos turns now one way and now the other. In the age of Cronus a god tended human beings and the earth produced food of itself. In the present age the god has let go, the world runs backward, and people must care for themselves. The myth shows that the king they are defining is a human ruler of a human herd, not a divine shepherd. The stranger then takes weaving as a model. Weaving is easy to separate from the neighbouring arts of carding, spinning, and fulling, and it shows what a definition should look like. He distinguishes causes from contributory causes, and he introduces two kinds of measurement, one that compares greater and smaller with each other and one that measures them against due measure. Constitutions are then ranked. The true constitution is the one in which the person who knows rules, whether by law or without it, and whether the ruled consent or not. Monarchy, aristocracy, and democracy are imitations, better when they keep to law and worse when they do not. The statesman's own work is a kind of weaving. He binds the courageous natures and the moderate natures together, by education and by marriages, so that the city does not tear itself between rashness and caution."
    ),
    "apology": (
        "Socrates speaks to the jury of five hundred and one Athenians. He answers first the old accusers, the ones who have been saying for years that he studies things in the sky and under the earth and makes the weaker argument the stronger. He recalls the comic Socrates of Aristophanes. The source of the reputation, he says, was Chaerephon's question to the oracle at Delphi, which answered that no one was wiser than Socrates. He tested the oracle by questioning politicians, poets, and craftsmen, and found that each group thought it knew what it did not. He concluded that his wisdom was knowing that he did not know. He then answers Meletus, who has charged him, with Anytus and Lycon, of corrupting the young and of not recognising the gods of the city. He shows that Meletus has never cared about the education of the young, and that the charge about gods contradicts itself if Socrates believes in divine signs at all. He refuses to stop philosophising in exchange for acquittal. He mentions Potidaea, Amphipolis, and Delium, his stand against the illegal trial of the generals, and his refusal under the Thirty to arrest Leon of Salamis. He will not parade his sons and weep. The vote goes against him. Offered the chance to name a penalty, he says he deserves free meals in the Prytaneum, then, with his friends pledging the money, offers thirty minas. The jury sentences him to death. He tells those who condemned him that killing him will not stop the questions, and those who acquitted him that death is either a dreamless sleep or a move to a place where he can talk with the great dead. It is time to go, he to die and they to live."
    ),
    "crito": (
        "It is just before dawn in the prison. Crito has come early, bribed the guard, and is sitting by Socrates, surprised to find him still asleep. The sacred ship has been sighted off Sunium on its return from Delos. When it arrives, the executions delayed for the festival will go ahead, so Socrates is likely to die the day after tomorrow. Crito has arranged the escape. The informers will not be expensive, and friends abroad, including Simmias in Thebes and Cebes, have money ready. He says Socrates will leave his sons uneducated, and that Crito himself will look cowardly for having failed to save him. Socrates says he will not leave unless Crito can show that leaving is just. He will not follow the opinion of the many, only the opinion of the one who understands, as a man in training follows his doctor. He imagines the laws of Athens standing before him. They say they begat him, reared him, and gave him a share of every good thing. He was free to leave the city if he disliked its arrangements, and he has stayed seventy years. To run away now, against a verdict, would be to try to destroy the laws. Nor should he drag his friends into danger. Crito has nothing left to say. Socrates tells him to give up, and to follow the way the god is leading."
    ),
    "phaedo": (
        "Echecrates of Phlius asks Phaedo, who was there, to tell him how Socrates died. Phaedo describes the morning in the prison. The friends were let in early. Xanthippe was sitting with their little boy. Socrates asked Crito to have her taken home, and she left crying. Socrates, rubbing the leg from which the chain had just been undone, remarked on how pleasure and pain follow each other. He said a dream had ordered him to make music, and that he had been writing verses in case philosophy was not the music meant. Cebes asked, on behalf of the poet Evenus, why Socrates was willing to die. Socrates answered that a philosopher spends his life preparing for the separation of soul from body. Simmias and Cebes demand proofs. Socrates argues that the living come from the dead, that learning is recollection, and that the soul is more like the unseen than like the body. Simmias objects that the soul may be only the body's attunement. Cebes objects that it may outlast several bodies and still die at last. Socrates tells of abandoning the natural philosophers for an explanation by forms, the beautiful by beauty itself, and he answers both objections. A myth follows, of a great earth, of hollows where we live, and of the rivers that carry souls. He bathes so the women will not have to wash the body, sees his three children, and sends them away. The officer of the Eleven comes in weeping. The poison is brought. Socrates asks what one is supposed to do, drinks it, and walks until his legs grow heavy. He lies down, covers his face, and uncovers it once to tell Crito they owe a cock to Asclepius. Crito closes his mouth and his eyes."
    ),
    "philebus": (
        "Socrates is arguing with Philebus, who has maintained that pleasure is the good, and with Protarchus, who has taken over Philebus' side of the debate. They agree to look for the state of soul that can make a human life happy. Socrates says that if pleasure is the whole good, a life of the greatest pleasures with no thought would do, and if wisdom is the whole good, a life of pure thought with no feeling would do. Protarchus admits he would not choose a pleasure that he could not perceive, and Socrates admits that no one would choose a wisdom that never felt joy. So the good life is a mixture. They then ask what kind of mixture, and which ingredient is more responsible for its goodness. Socrates sets out four kinds: the unlimited, the limit, the mixture of the two, and the cause of the mixture. Pleasure belongs mostly with the unlimited. Reason is on the side of the cause. He argues that some pleasures are false, because they rest on a false belief about what is to come, and that the violent pleasures are mixed with pain. The pure pleasures, such as those of learning and of beautiful colours and sounds, are the ones fit to be admitted. At the end they rank the goods. Measure and proportion come first, then reason and intelligence, then the sciences, and the pure pleasures after them. Philebus' candidate, bodily pleasure, comes last among the things allowed into the good life."
    ),
    "hipparchus": (
        "Socrates is talking with a friend who has just spoken scornfully of lovers of gain. Socrates asks what the lover of gain loves. Gain, they agree, is the opposite of loss, and a loss is being deprived of something good. So gain is something good, and the lover of gain loves the good. But then everyone is a lover of gain, since everyone wants what benefits him, and the reproach makes no sense. The friend resists. Socrates tells him about Hipparchus, son of Peisistratus, who ruled Athens, educated the citizens, brought the poet Anacreon to the city, and set up herms on the roads inscribed with wise sayings of his own, so that people would admire his wisdom rather than the Delphic maxims. Hipparchus was later killed by Harmodius and Aristogeiton. The story is meant to show a man who loved gain of a respectable kind, the gain of being thought wise. The friend is not persuaded. They circle back over the same argument, and Socrates concludes that they should not slander the lover of gain. The friend agrees only that they should say what they think, and the piece ends there."
    ),
    "lovers": (
        "Socrates goes into the school of Dionysius the grammarian and finds two boys arguing, drawing circles and imitating the angles with their hands, apparently over Anaxagoras or Oenopides. He asks the young man beside him, a lover of one of the boys, what they are so serious about. The lover says it is idle chatter about things in the sky. Another young man, a rival for the same boy and a student of music, interrupts and says philosophy is a fine thing. Socrates asks them what philosophy is. The athletic lover treats it as a smattering of many arts, enough to follow a conversation and not enough to be a craftsman. Socrates compares that person to the pentathlete, who loses to the specialist in each event. He asks what knowledge would let someone assign each specialist his work and judge whether it is well done. They arrive at a knowledge of how to make human beings better, the knowledge a good ruler would have, and they agree that this, rather than dabbling, is what philosophy comes to. Socrates leaves the point there."
    ),
    "cleitophon": (
        "Socrates asks Cleitophon whether he has heard rightly that Cleitophon, in talks with Lysias, criticises Socrates' conversations and praises the teaching of Thrasymachus. Cleitophon says he has been partly critical, and he asks leave to set the criticism out. Socrates agrees. Cleitophon then speaks at length and Socrates does not answer. He grants that Socrates is unmatched at waking people up, rebuking them for caring about money rather than about virtue, and turning them toward justice. What he cannot get, he says, is the next step. When he asked Socrates' companions what justice is and how one acquires it, they told him that justice is the beneficial, or friendship in cities, or the same thing named again. None of them could say what the product of justice is, the way a doctor can say that the product of medicine is health. He challenged Socrates himself and got nothing straight. He is ready to be Socrates' pupil if someone will teach him. Otherwise he will go to Thrasymachus and the others who claim they can. The dialogue ends on that speech."
    ),
    "minos": (
        "Socrates asks a companion what law is. The companion says it is the things that are accepted as lawful. Socrates points out that speech is not the things that are spoken, nor sight the things that are seen, and he asks again. The companion says law is the resolution of a city. Socrates answers that some resolutions are good and some are wicked, while law is meant to be something worthy, and that different cities and the same city at different times resolve opposite things, although what is real does not change in that way. He compares law to the finding of a doctor or a farmer who knows the subject. Law, he says, wants to be the discovery of what is. The companion is startled to hear Minos, the Cretan king, praised, because tragedy presents him as savage. Socrates says Minos conversed every nine years with Zeus, and that he gave Crete the laws which Lycurgus later studied for Sparta. Homer and Hesiod praise him. The Athenian tragedians slandered him because Athens had gone to war with Crete. The companion asks what makes a good lawgiver. Socrates says the one who can sow virtue in souls. The talk breaks off as he returns to who that person is."
    ),
    "laws": (
        "Three old men walk the long road in Crete from Cnossus toward the cave and temple of Zeus. They are an Athenian stranger, Clinias of Crete, and Megillus of Sparta. The Athenian asks whether a god or a man is given the credit for their laws, and the talk opens into a day-long argument about what law is for. Clinias says the laws of Crete aim at victory in war, because every city is always at war with every other. The Athenian answers that the best law looks to the whole of virtue, not to courage alone, and that peace, not war, is the serious thing. He even defends supervised drinking as a way of testing character, and he uses the histories of Persia and of Athens to show how freedom and authority have to be held together. Clinias then admits he has been appointed, with nine others, to frame laws for a new colony, and he asks the Athenian to build the city in speech with him. They fix the households, the land, marriage, schooling, and the officials. Every law is to open with a prelude, so that a citizen obeys because he understands and not only because he is afraid. Later the Athenian argues, against those who say the world is only matter, that soul, which moves itself, is older than body, and that the gods care for human affairs. He sets out the punishments for impiety, theft, and murder. The work ends with a nocturnal council of the oldest and the best, trained in mathematics and in the single aim of the laws, and with the question of how that council is to be given the power to keep the city pointed at virtue."
    ),
    "epinomis": (
        "The Athenian stranger, Clinias, and Megillus meet again, as they had agreed, to say what knowledge a mortal needs if he is to be wise. Clinias recalls that they had promised to find the study which makes a human being good. The Athenian reviews the arts that people ordinarily admire. Farming, building, hunting, imitation, medicine, navigation, and generalship are all useful, and none of them is wisdom. He turns to number. A creature that could not count could not be brave, temperate, or just, and would have no account to give of anything. Number was given to human beings so that they could think. The highest use of it is the study of the heavens, the movements of the stars, which are the fairest and most orderly of visible things and are themselves divine. The Athenian describes the regions of the cosmos and the living beings proper to each, down to the daimons who mediate between gods and humans. Wisdom, he concludes, is piety instructed by astronomy. Without that study the guardian council they had planned will not have the knowledge it needs. The dialogue ends with Clinias agreeing that the stranger must be made to join the council."
    ),
    "letters": (
        "Thirteen letters are preserved under Plato's name, most of them addressed to Dionysius II, tyrant of Syracuse, or to Dion, Plato's friend at that court, and a few to other Greeks of the same years. They are not a single conversation, and they are not all of one quality. The Seventh is the long one. It answers the friends of Dion, who has been murdered, and it tells Plato's own story: his disillusion with the Thirty Tyrants in Athens, his judgment that the cities of his time were all badly governed, and his repeated visits to Syracuse, first under Dionysius I and then, at Dion's urging, under Dionysius II. He describes the philosophical test he set the younger Dionysius, the ruler's shallow enthusiasm, the quarrels, Plato's detention in the palace, and his eventual escape. In the middle of the narrative he pauses to say what he has never written. The things he really cares about cannot be put in a book. Understanding is kindled in the soul by long companionship, suddenly, like a light struck from a fire. He also warns Dion's party against taking revenge in a way that would repeat the disease they had meant to cure. The Eighth letter continues that political advice, urging a compromise in Syracuse among royal power, law, and freedom. The shorter letters give recommendations, rebukes, and requests. Several of them are generally thought to be later school exercises written in Plato's name."
    ),
}
